	Gray clouds scudded over the skies as the riders came to 
the Duali Tribe camp.  I had just finished giving water to the horses
when the men in the camp were called before the elders.
	"Great evil has befallen the world.  An artifact of great
importance to mighty Crom has been stolen and hidden away in the 
depths of hell itself.  The Amulet of Yendor must be retrieved!"
	Bones were cast.  I was chosen.
	Despite our fearsome heritage, I must admit, I was more than
a little worried as I prepared for the quest.  I took only my trusty
axe, my armor, and some food.  As I approached the stinking caverns 
into which the thief fled, a small grey kitten began to follow me.
	Echoes of grunts, and hissing whispers filled my ears as I began
to explore this place.  Gold was plentiful.  I began to carry it in my
pack until I could hardly bear to walk.  It was then I decided that my
greed would have to be quashed if I were to survive.  Occasionally I 
would find a pretty stone, and I thought of my woman, Katrina, at camp.
I was sure I would never see her again.
	Deeper into the dungeons I went.  I could feel my doom sealing 
upon me with the mounds of rock above me.  I reached a branch and went 
deeper and found an actively worked mine.  Many hordes of little gnomes
came after me, but they were only a nuisance.  I picked up potions, and
wands.  I did not use them, at least initially.  We of the Duali Tribe
do not trust such mystic ways.  Books were discarded, but I shunned them.
	I came to a mining town.  It was here that I began to understand
the mighty task before me.  In the center of town was a temple of Crom!
I spent a long time, conferring with the priest there.  I gave him what
money I'd discovered, and I felt the glow and protection of Crom himself
surrounding me!  Likewise, I was able to begin to understand which of
the strange artifacts around me were safe, and I was taught some of 
their mysteries.  I cleared the town of some riffraff and brought the 
bloody corpses to my priest, and he directed me in the ways of sacrifice
and piety.  In this way I became closer to Crom.  He awarded me with 
a sacred axe named Cleaver.  With further devotion, I was awarded a blade
that burned red with heat, but did not scorch my hand when I held it.
I named it Firebrand.
	After a time, I took my leave of the priest.  Deeper into the 
Gnomish Mines (as I had come to think of them).  Finally I reached the
depths and found wondrous pretties, as well as a strange gray stone.  
Holding it, I could feel the winds of Fate beginning to blow my way.
	I returned to Minetown, not for the last time, but probably the
happiest.  When I would return again, I would be covered in gore and 
raving.  Back to my doom and up I went, and then down into the regular
tunnels and warrens.  Stranger and stranger creatures I met, and 
subsequently slayed.  My skill became as sharp as the burning sword I
held in my hand.  The strange kitten who followed me had gotten lost 
long ago, and I felt no need to wheedle another beast to be at my side.
	I tarried not with the woman who spoke of strange things, 
secrets only she could see.  In truth, I could understand nothing of
what she said, so gave her none of my collected gold.  Another branching
and I was baffled.  Boulders everywhere.  I sat and puzzled, and realized
I could just give them a little push and create the safe way through
this maze.  Four levels of this and then I discovered a vast treasure
trove.  The beasts were no problem, and I discovered a strange bag and
the word Elbereth.  I shunned the word.  It was in the disgusting 
Elvish tongue and I would not soil myself by using it.  The bag however
could hold any amount of things I put into it.  It was big enough 
inside I could almost hide in it myself, though I was afraid I 
might get lost.  Using a trick the priest taught me, I quickly 
blessed it, to drive away the demons of magic inside, and the 
bag got even lighter.  What a find!
	Only a few more levels and I discovered that the priests
were attempting to bring enlightenment to the horde of idiotic beasts.
Three shrines I found, one each for Set, Crom, and Mitra.  In Crom's 
I made the floors run a rainbow of colors as I sacrificed mightily. 
Nothing that attacked me was safe, though I stayed my hand from
the creatures who bore me no ill will.  Crom gave me the blessed
twin of Firebrand, a blade that burned, not with fire, but with 
the soul sucking chill of winter.  Also I had watched carefully 
at the monsters who surrounded me, and learned some of the secrets 
of magic.  Sticks caused deadly things to happen.  Flasks could
create many effects.  
	Then a strange thing occurred.  I heard a voice in my head,
one of the leaders of the Duali Tribe from which I came.  In a flash
I was transported to home... or was I?  I could not wander far, and
I believed this was a trick!  After more searching in the dungeon 
and gaining more experience I came back and understood.  Pelias told
me of the thief Thoth Amon.  I undertook the quest to slay him, and 
slay him I did.  I retrieved the holy Heart of Ahriman and a strange
mystic bell.  Pelias granted me permission to carry the Heart and
I kept it close.  The bell I took with me as I was told it would aid
me in the final moments of my original quest.
	Strange things I saw, and stranger beasts I slew.  In a
magical fortress, I slew soldiers and wicked beasts, dragons and
goo.  I had found previously a throneroom.  In a fit of impudence,
I sat on it, and miraculously was given a wish!  The priest of Crom
had taught me what to concentrate on, and behold, a princely 
mail of silver scales was in my arms.  It was light and hardly nothing
would penetrate.  Beams of evil would reflect from its perfect surface!
It served me well in this fort.  Dragons railed their wicked gasses
and bolts but it spanged off my chest with no effort.  I finally
slew every living being in the place, and stole all the gold and 
gems.  The gold I gave to Crom.  The gems I stored in my pack in
case I returned to Katrina. 
	Licking the blood from my blade (and no, my tongue did NOT
stick to Frostbrand!) I journeyed on.  In a swampy mess, I came
across the mythical Medusa.  My mail once more came to my aid and
she was frozen in time as stone.  Unfortunately for her, I did not
let her be preserved and hacked her apart as soon as I could, 
discovering yet another path deeper into the dungeons.  
	Here was a castle.  I slew soldiers at the front gates and
tooted one of their horns, until they let down the bridge.  The fools!
In a flash I was upon them and fought upon a growing mound of 
bodies outside the front gates until I could win my way in deeper.
In a tower of the castle I found the most wondrous thing I could 
imagine, a wand that would grant wishes.  At first I thought I would
wish myself back to Katrina, the quest uncompleted.  However, my
sense of duty to Crom took hold, and I imagined myself as the
fastest, most powerful, most brilliant barbarian in the world.
Soon I was equipped for the battle of my life.  
	If I had thought that the dungeons were evil, I had
no conception of the chaos beneath that castle.  Hordes of the
dead came at me, trying to suck the life out of me in pitiful
jealousy.  At the end, a new priest, not one of the triad,
greeted me.  I shunned this man, sacrificing and praying to
the usurper whom I shall not name.
	Below I was greeted with a warren of baffling mazes.
Forsooth, if it were not for my brilliance granted with my
helm I'm sure I would not have been able to understand them!
A demon named Asmodeus came, and I payed his fee.  What is 
gold to me?  In another evil pestilential swamp I slew the
demon Jubilex.  I paid another toll to Beelzebub, and then
in a town for demons, I swung the strange, dangerous creature
cockatrice at the black fiend, Orcus, and turned him into a
statue.
	Deeper I went, and avoided traps left and right.  I read a
piece of paper and I was teleported to the place I began!  I went
mad for a time.  I hacked at the walls, aiming to go a crow's path 
to each stairs.  I reached Minetown and I slew all there, save
for a kindly gentleman who sold candles.  His life I spared, and
the priest who taught me.  Everyone else was forfeit.
	Soon I was back in Gehennom, and I retrieved the Candelabra
from Vlad the Impaler.  I had thought him dead many centuries, but these
tunnels are revealing their hideous secrets to me finally.  I came
upon the one who stole the amulet in the first place, Wizard Rodney.
and I slew him with Frostbrand.  I took his Book and ran to the depths
where I knew the entrance to the final sanctum lay.
	The Candleabrum was lit.
	The Bell was rung.
	The Book was read.
	Heaven and earth were shaken from the convergence of such
power and the stairs to He-Who-Shall-Not-Be-Named's Sanctum appeared.  
	I burned with purpose as I slashed right and left, carving my 
through yet another hellish graveyard, even seeing a ghost of myself
as a taunt that I would never survive.  Rodney came back; hadn't 
I killed him?  Well no matter, I did it again, and would do it
more.  I took the Amulet, the precious trinket of Gods and ran.
	I ran as only a Barbarian can, in leaps and bounds,
dodging beasts, shunning killing them unless they blocked my
ascent.  I screamed my beloved's name, Katrina, as went higher 
and higher, revisting the gory scenes I had created as if in a previous
life.  I slew that Wizard again and again, and finally I reached the exit.
	But as I emerged, I was not where I thought I would come
out.  I knew then I was a pawn for the Gods.  I had one more part
to play.  I suffered the ashes of Gehennom, and now I tasted the dust of
the Plane of Earth.  After winning my way through living stones, I was 
in the air, dodging and swooping.  In the Plane of Fire, I laughed, 
quite mad by now I'm sure, leaping over spitting fumes, hovering over 
lava that would form worlds yet unknown.  In the Plane of Water I 
rested, until I was ready.
	Then, the plane of the Gods, the Astral Plane.  I was granted 
a guardian angel, but I was too prideful to stand behind such a glorious 
being.  I was the one who retrieved the Amulet!  I would lead
the charge.  I slashed and crammed myself through crowding
bodies.  I beamed my death stick repeatedly, getting rid of the
evil Pestilence more than once.  My life was running out.  I
aimed for an altar.  I won the doorway.  I leapt off the corpse
of a red dragon and landed on the blood slick surface.  I felt 
the markings and it was Crom's altar!  I stood and raised the Amulet
and gave it and myself to Crom!

